cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software or its bundled corpora, please cite it as
  below. See CITATIONS.md for citing the individual data sources
  (yajnadevam/indus-website, mayig/indus-valley-script-corpus,
  indusscript.in/M77, ETCSL, the Digital Corpus of Sanskrit, and the
  Sangam Literature Corpus), which is separate from citing this
  toolkit itself.
title: >-
  Indus Script Computational Analysis Toolkit: A Falsification-Oriented
  Statistical Framework
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Bose
    given-names: Joy
repository-code: 'https://github.com/joyboseroy/indus_decipher'
license: MIT
keywords:
  - indus script
  - indus valley civilization
  - undeciphered scripts
  - computational epigraphy
  - historical linguistics
  - falsification testing
  - null models
abstract: >-
  A statistical, falsification-oriented toolkit for computational
  analysis of the undeciphered Indus Valley script: entropy and n-gram
  replication of the published literature, a synthetic-civilization
  falsification harness, adversarial and stratified null models,
  substitution-graph and distributional-embedding analysis, and
  external-language calibration against real Sumerian, Sanskrit, and
  Old Tamil corpora. Developed with Claude (Anthropic) doing
  implementation, debugging, and drafting under human direction and
  review at every step (see the repository's README, "How this was
  built"); this file's author metadata reflects the human research
  direction, not the implementation labor, and anyone citing this work
  should feel free to describe that division of labor explicitly rather
  than assume a conventional single-author model applies.
